## PART 9 — ACTUAL DATA ANALYSIS

**Customer profile:** 3,900 customers, ages 18–70 (mean 44.1, median 44). Gender split is **68% male (2,652) / 32% female (1,248)** — not balanced.

**Spend profile:** Purchase amounts range $20–$100 (mean $59.76, median $60). Total revenue across the dataset is **$233,081**. Spend is fairly tight and evenly distributed — there is no extreme high-spender outlier group (max is only $100).

**The standout pattern — Gender vs. Subscription/Discount:**
- **0 of 1,248 female customers (0%)** have `Subscription Status = Yes`.
- **0 of 1,248 female customers (0%)** have `Discount Applied = Yes` or `Promo Code Used = Yes`.
- **39.7% of male customers (1,053 of 2,652)** have an active subscription.
- **63.2% of male customers (1,677 of 2,652)** have used a discount/promo code.

This is a complete, categorical split — not a slight tendency. It is the single most important — and most unusual — finding in the dataset. It is statistically implausible for a real-world retailer to have literally zero female subscribers or discount users, so this most likely reflects **how the dataset was constructed/labeled** (a data quality / structural artifact) rather than genuine customer behavior. It is reported here as a finding either way, since it is what the data actually shows, but it should be flagged rather than assumed to be a market truth.

**Category performance:** Clothing dominates by volume (1,737 transactions, 44.5% of all purchases, $104,264 revenue), followed by Accessories (1,240 transactions, $74,200), Footwear (599 transactions, $36,093), and Outerwear (324 transactions, $18,524). However, **average spend per transaction is nearly identical across categories** (Footwear $60.26, Clothing $60.03, Accessories $59.84, Outerwear $57.17) — category differences are driven by transaction volume, not by basket size.

**Top revenue items:** Blouse ($10,410), Shirt ($10,332), Dress ($10,320), Pants ($10,090), Jewelry ($10,010) — the top 5 items are within 4% of each other, indicating no single dominant product.

**Subscription and discount effect on spend:** Subscribers spend $59.49 on average vs. $59.87 for non-subscribers — **essentially no difference**. Customers who used a discount spent $59.28 vs. $60.13 for those who didn't — again **no meaningful difference**. Subscribers average 26.08 previous purchases vs. 25.08 for non-subscribers — a small (~1 purchase) gap that is not practically significant given the range is 1–50.

**Review ratings:** Average rating is 3.75/5 overall. Category ratings are nearly flat (Footwear 3.79, Accessories 3.77, Outerwear 3.75, Clothing 3.72) — no category stands out as a satisfaction problem or strength. Ratings are identical (3.75) for subscribers and non-subscribers.

**Seasonal spend:** Fall has the highest average spend ($61.56), followed by Winter ($60.36), Spring ($58.74), and Summer ($58.41) — a modest ~$3 range, not a dramatic seasonal effect.

**Age groups:** Average spend by age bucket ranges narrowly from $58.88 (66+) to $60.65 (18–25) — **age shows almost no relationship with spend**.

**Correlation check:** Age, Purchase Amount, Review Rating, and Previous Purchases show near-zero pairwise correlations (all |r| < 0.05). **There is no meaningful linear relationship among the numeric variables in this dataset.**

**Geographic spread:** Purchases are spread almost evenly across all 50 US states (65–96 transactions per state, ~78 average) — no state dominates. Average spend by state ranges from $54.18 (Connecticut) to $67.60 (Alaska), but sample sizes per state (~60–95) are too small to draw confident regional conclusions.

**Size and demographics:** Medium is the most common size across every category (45% of all purchases overall), with no notable size-preference difference by category.

**Missing data pattern:** All 37 missing Review Ratings belong to male customers, concentrated somewhat more in Clothing (19 of 37). Given the small count, this is noted as a data quality observation rather than a business insight.

**Payment/shipping methods:** Both are close to evenly distributed (Payment Method: PayPal 17.4%, Credit Card 17.2%, Cash 17.2%, Debit Card 16.3%, Venmo 16.3%, Bank Transfer 15.7%; Shipping Type: Free Shipping 17.3%, Standard 16.8%, Store Pickup 16.7%, Next Day Air 16.6%, Express 16.6%, 2-Day Shipping 16.1%) — customers are not concentrated on any single option.

